13:38:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair point about butcher shops in general — blood, sawdust, bits of trimmed fat and bone are just part of the job. Butchery is inherently messy work: you're cutting through muscle, fat, and connective tissue, sawdust or wood shavings are often put down to absorb blood and grease, and aprons get stained no matter how careful someone is. That's true regardless of who's doing the cutting. But notice that this description doesn't actually tell us anything about a gender difference — it's describing the trade itself, not comparing male vs. female butchers' work areas. If every counter you've seen looks similar, and most butchers you've encountered are men (which is still the norm in the industry in a lot of places), then what you're observing is "butchering is messy," not "men make more mess than women would." To actually test the original claim, you'd need to compare workspaces at similar stages of the same tasks, done by different people, controlling for role and workload — which is basically impossible to do informally by eyeballing shops. Without that kind of comparison, it's hard to say there's a real gender effect versus just the nature of the trade itself.
