13:38:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a much more solid observation, and yes — the association between butchery and mess has real, practical roots: - **Blood and viscera are unavoidable** – you can't cut into flesh and bone without fluids and tissue getting on surfaces, tools, and clothing. - **Sawdust/shavings on floors** was standard practice for a long time (and still is in some traditional shops) specifically because it absorbs blood and grease, making floors less slippery and easier to sweep out at the end of the day. It looks messy but it's actually a hygiene/safety measure. - **Fast-paced, hands-on work** – butchering involves constant physical handling of raw product, so aprons and surfaces get dirty quickly no matter how skilled or careful the person is. - **Historical trade image** – butchers have long been associated in cultural memory with blood and grit specifically because the job was visible to the public (open shopfronts, market stalls) in a way that, say, a tanner's more disturbing work wasn't. So the saying almost certainly reflects the visible, unavoidable byproducts of the trade rather than sloppiness or lack of professionalism. In fact, a lot of what looks "dirty" (bloody aprons, sawdust, bits of trim) coexists with quite rigorous hygiene standards behind the scenes — equipment sanitizing, temperature control, separate cutting boards for different meats, etc. The mess is surface-level and constant; it doesn't necessarily mean the operation is unclean in a food-safety sense.
